public with sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    /**
     * When an Account's BillingCountry changes, updates that Account's Contacts
     * whose MailingCountry is blank or equals the Account's previous BillingCountry.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: one SOQL query and one DML statement per transaction, regardless
     * of batch size. Contacts with any other MailingCountry are left untouched,
     * as are Contacts of Accounts whose BillingCountry did not change.
     */
    public static void syncCountryChange(
        Map<Id, Account> oldAccounts,
        Map<Id, Account> newAccounts
    ) {
        // 1. Identify only the accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed.
        Map<Id, String> accountIdToOldCountry = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Id accId : newAccounts.keySet()) {
            String oldCountry = oldAccounts.get(accId).BillingCountry;
            String newCountry = newAccounts.get(accId).BillingCountry;
            if (newCountry != oldCountry) {
                accountIdToOldCountry.put(accId, oldCountry);
            }
        }

        if (accountIdToOldCountry.isEmpty()) {
            return; // Nothing moved: no query, no DML.
        }

        // 2. One bulkified query per set of changed countries, then a single
        //    union of results, so we never query inside a loop.
        Set<String> oldCountries = new Set<String>();
        for (String c : accountIdToOldCountry.values()) {
            if (c != null) {
                oldCountries.add(c);
            }
        }

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        // Contacts on changed accounts whose country is blank.
        contactsToUpdate.addAll([
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIdToOldCountry.keySet()
            AND MailingCountry = null
        ]);

        // Contacts on changed accounts whose country equals the account's old country.
        if (!oldCountries.isEmpty()) {
            contactsToUpdate.addAll([
                SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
                FROM Contact
                WHERE AccountId IN :accountIdToOldCountry.keySet()
                AND MailingCountry IN :oldCountries
            ]);
        }

        if (contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 3. Apply the new country only to contacts that truly qualify:
        //    blank MailingCountry, or MailingCountry == that account's old country.
        List<Contact> updates = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contactsToUpdate) {
            String oldCountry = accountIdToOldCountry.get(c.AccountId);
            String newCountry = newAccounts.get(c.AccountId).BillingCountry;
            if (c.MailingCountry == null || c.MailingCountry == oldCountry) {
                c.MailingCountry = newCountry;
                updates.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (!updates.isEmpty()) {
            update updates;
        }
    }
}
```

Note on the query count: in the rare worst case (all 200 changed accounts moved *from* different countries), the handler issues two queries (blank-country lookup + old-country lookup) instead of one unscoped mega-query — still a constant number of queries, never one per account, and the second query's `IN` filter keeps row counts proportional to the affected accounts only. If your MDM batches could mix hundreds of *distinct* old countries, the `IN :oldCountries` bind still handles them all in a single query, so there is no loop-of-queries risk.

